{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00400 - MONDO_5984",
  "verbalization": "Griseofulvin, a fungal antifungal agent, is implicated in the treatment of Tinea Pedis through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Griseofulvin may be repurposed for Tinea Pedis, as it is associated with Tinea Barbae and Tinea Unguium, both of which are fungal infections. These conditions are further linked to other agents, such as Tolnaftate and Ciclopirox, which are also used in the treatment of Tinea Pedis. The mechanism of action for Griseofulvin is primarily antifungal, targeting the fungal cell membrane and inhibiting microtubule formation, which is a well-documented mode of action in antifungal drugs. The integration of these pathways implies that Griseofulvin may exert its antifungal effects through a similar mechanism, potentially offering a therapeutic option for Tinea Pedis. The evidence is supported by a moderate level of confidence, with the strongest link being the direct indication between Griseofulvin and Tinea Pedis, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
